The sort itself is fine, so the bug is almost certainly in the comparison or the input handling. The classic culprit is using `==` on the two char arrays instead of `Arrays.equals`, which compares references and is always false for two different arrays. If they did call `Arrays.equals`, then the other likely cause is case or whitespace, since `Arrays.sort` is case-sensitive and `"Listen"` sorts differently from `"listen"`.

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

public boolean isAnagram(String a, String b) {
    if (a == null || b == null) return false;
    char[] x = a.toLowerCase().replaceAll("\\s+", "").toCharArray();
    char[] y = b.toLowerCase().replaceAll("\\s+", "").toCharArray();
    if (x.length != y.length) return false;
    Arrays.sort(x);
    Arrays.sort(y);
    return Arrays.equals(x, y);
}
```

`Arrays.equals` compares contents element by element, and normalizing case and whitespace first makes the check match what people actually mean by an anagram. That runs in O(n log n) from the sorts, with O(n) extra space for the arrays.

[[GIST]] use Arrays.equals, normalize case and spaces

The bug is `wg.Add(1)` inside the goroutine, so `Wait` can return before any goroutine runs and the slice stays zeroed. Increment before launching.

```go
func fetchAll(urls []string) []int {
	results := make([]int, len(urls))
	var wg sync.WaitGroup
	for i, u := range urls {
		wg.Add(1)
		go func(i int, u string) {
			defer wg.Done()
			results[i] = fetchStatus(u)
		}(i, u)
	}
	wg.Wait()
	return results
}
```

Now the counter is set before `Wait`, so every write lands. Writing distinct indices concurrently is safe.

