def merge_windows(windows, grace):
    # 1. Report the first inverted window in original (pre-sort) order.
    for i, (start, end) in enumerate(windows):
        if end < start:
            raise ValueError(f"inverted window at index {i}")

    # 2. Sort by start on a copy; build fresh pairs so the input isn't mutated.
    merged = []
    for start, end in sorted(windows):
        if end == start:            # zero-length window: dropped, never bridges
            continue
        if merged and start <= merged[-1][1] + grace:
            if end > merged[-1][1]:  # grace decides merging, never extends
                merged[-1][1] = end
        else:
            merged.append([start, end])
    return merged
```

Inverted windows are rejected first in input order, then the list is sorted by start and each window folds into the last merged one when `start <= end + grace`, with zero-length windows skipped before they can bridge anything. Complexity is O(n log n) time and O(n) space for the sorted copy and result.
